{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Before all his brothers he fell.</b> Elsewhere it says ״יִשְׁכֹּן״ (Genesis 16:12). Rashi explained: while Abraham had not yet died, he would dwell (יִשְׁכֹּן); once Abraham died, he fell (נָפָל). But if we accept that Ishmael repented after Abraham's death — for we find no repentance of his other than that he let Isaac go before him — then the reverse is what he should have said: during Abraham's lifetime he was fit to fall, and not at his death, for after that he repented, so why should he fall? But look to the right and see: every instance of ״נָפָל״ connotes the past, and ״יִשְׁכֹּן״ connotes the future. If so, certainly the meaning of ״נָפָל״ — in the past tense — is before he repented, and ״יִשְׁכֹּן״ — in the future tense — to say that he is destined to dwell after he repents. And we may explain ״נָפָל״ in the language of submission, for so is the way of everyone who submits himself: he falls before every man. Just as he began to fall before Isaac — although he was older than him, he nevertheless let him go before him and submitted to him — so too he submitted before all his brothers, and that is the meaning of 'falling.' And through this Rashi's difficulty is also resolved: before he repented, ״יִשְׁכֹּן״ — for he did not submit; and after he repented, ״נָפָל״ — that is to say, he submitted before all his brothers, and this falling is his remedy."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:59.667203180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}